Jesus' family lineage
This is the genealogy of Jesus Christ. He is the son of David. He is also the son of Abraham.
Abraham was the father of Isaac. Isaac was the father of Jacob. Jacob was the father of Judah and his brothers.
Judah the father of Perez and Zerah, and Tamar their mother. And Perez the father of Hezron. And Hezron the father of Ram.
Ram was the father of Amminadab, and Amminadab was the father of Nahshon, and Nahshon was the father of Salmon.
Salmon was the father of Boaz. Rahab was the mother of Boaz. Boaz was the father of Obed. Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.
And Jesse the father of king David. And David the father of Solomon. And the mother of Solomon was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon was the father of Rehoboam. And Rehoboam was the father of Abijah. And Abijah was the father of Asa.
Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. And Jehoshaphat was the father of Joram. And Joram was the father of Uzziah.
Uzziah is the father of Jotham. Jotham is the father of Ahaz. Ahaz is the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah is the father of Manasseh. Manasseh is the father of Amon. Amon is the father of Josiah.
Josiah was the father of Jehoiachin and his brothers. And in those days the Jewish people were forced to go to Babylon.
After that, the family line continued. Jehoiachin was the father of Shealtiel. Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel begat Abiud. Abiud begat Eliakim. Eliakim begat Azor.
Azur was the father of Zadok. Zadok was the father of Akim. Akim was the father of Eliud.
Eliud was the father of Eliezer. Eliezer was the father of Mathan. Mathan was the father of Jacob.
Jacob was the father of Joseph, the husband of Mary. Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
So, there were 14 generations from Abraham to David. And there were 14 from David until the Jewish people had to go to Babylon. And there were 14 from that time to Christ, the birth of Jesus Christ.
And so the birth of Jesus Christ came to pass. His mother was Mary and his betrothed was Joseph. They had married, but before they lived together, it became clear that she was pregnant. She was overshadowed by the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband, Yusuf, was a righteous man. He did not wish to shame her publicly. Therefore, he intended to secretly divorce her.
But while Joseph was pondering this, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. The angel said, "Joseph, son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary as your wife. For the child conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
She will give birth to a son. You must name him Jesus. This is because he will save his people from their sins."
All this was to be fulfilled, what the Lord had said would happen. He had said through the prophet,
The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son, and he will be called Immanuel. The name Immanuel means "God with us."
Joseph awoke. He did what the angel of the Lord commanded him. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But he did not have relations with her until she gave birth to a son. And Joseph called his name Jesus.
The Visit of the Wise Men to Jesus
Jesus was born in Bethlehem in Judea. This happened in the time of Herod, the king of Judea. After the birth of Jesus, magi came from the east to Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the newborn king of the Jews? For we saw his star in the east and have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed, and all the people of Jerusalem were also troubled.
Herod gathered all the chief priests and the people. He also called the teachers of the law. He asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
"In Bethlehem of Judea," they answered him. "For thus it is written through the prophet: 'And you, Bethlehem, land of Judah, are by no means least among the rulers of Judah; for out of you will come a ruler who will shepherd my people Israel.'"
«As for you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, least of the clans of Judah, from you shall come forth for me one who will be a ruler of Israel, whose origin is from of old, from days of eternity.»
Then Herod secretly called the Magi. He found out from them the exact time the star appeared.
Send them to Bethlehem. And he said, "Go! And diligently search for the child. And when you find him, tell me. So that I may also go and worship him."
After the wise men had listened to the king, they set out on their journey. And the star which they had seen when it was in the east, went before them. And when it came to a stop over the place where the child was, they rejoiced with exceedingly great joy. And when they had come into the house, they saw the child with Mary his mother. And they fell down and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures and presented him with gifts of gold, frankincense, and myrrh. And being warned in a dream not to return to Herod, they departed for their own country by another route. Now after they had departed, an angel of the Lord appeared in a dream to Joseph, saying, "Get up, take the child and his mother, and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you, because Herod is about to search for the child to destroy him." So Joseph got up, took the child and his mother by night, and departed for Egypt. And they remained there until the death of Herod. This was the first Gospel according to Matthew.
When they saw the star, they were filled with joy.
The wise men went to the house. And there they saw the child with his mother Mary. So they bowed down and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. And they gave him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they went back to their own country by another road. Jesus' family flees to Egypt.
After the magicians went away, Joseph had a dream. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him. The angel said, "Get up! Take the boy and his mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return. Herod is going to look for the boy to kill him."
Joseph got up. And during the night, he went to Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
And they remained there until King Herod died. Then what the Lord had said through the prophet came true: "From Egypt I called my son." (Hosea 11:1)
Herod realized that the Magi had deceived him. He became furiously angry. He gave orders concerning Bethlehem and its vicinity. That all the male children who were two years old or less were to be killed. This coincided with the time that the Magi had seen the star.
And thus the word of the prophet Jeremiah, which he spoke, was fulfilled.
A voice was heard in Ramah, a voice of great weeping and bitter lamentation. Rachel weeping for her children, refusing to be comforted because they are no more. (Jeremiah 31:15) The return of Jesus' family to Nazareth.
After Herod's death, Joseph had a dream while he was still in Egypt. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Arise! Take the child and his mother. Go into the land of Israel. Those who were trying to kill the child are dead."
Joseph stood up, took the child and his mother Mary to the land of Israel.
But afterward he heard that Archelaus was ruler of Galilee. Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod. So Joseph became afraid to go there. God warned him in a dream, so he turned aside and went to the region of Galilee instead.
And there he lived in a city called Nazareth. And so what the prophets had said about Jesus came true. For they had said, "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way of the Lord.
In those days John the Baptist came and preached in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Repent, for the Kingdom of Heaven is at hand."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. He said, "A voice of one calling in the wilderness, 'Prepare the way for the Lord; make straight paths for him.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel's hair. He wore a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
The people went out to him from Jerusalem and all Judea. They also came from all the regions of the Jordan.
When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the River Jordan.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to the place where he was baptizing. And he said to them, "You brood of vipers, who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?"
Bring forth fruit that reveals you have truly turned away from your sins.
Do not suppose that you can say to yourselves, 'Abraham is our father.' I say to you, God is able from these very stones to raise up children to Abraham.
The axe is already laid at the roots of the trees. All the trees that do not bear good fruit will be cut down. They will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, calling you to repentance for your sins. But after me comes one who is stronger than I am. I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He baptizes you with the Holy Spirit and with fire.
Jesus teaches his disciples, explaining that they are blessed when people insult them, persecute them, and falsely say all kinds of evil against them because of Jesus. Jesus tells them to rejoice in such times, because their reward in heaven is great. Jesus warns them to be careful, however, because some will come using Jesus' name, claiming, "I am the Messiah," and they will deceive many people. Jesus tells his followers to be on guard, because they will hear of wars and rumors of wars, but these things must happen first, but they will not mean that the end has come yet. Jesus tells them that when they hear of wars and rumors of wars, they are to understand that these things must happen first, but they do not mean that the end has come yet. Jesus tells them that when these things begin to happen, they are to lift up their heads because their redemption is drawing near. Jesus speaks of a time when Jerusalem will be surrounded by armies, and those within the city walls will be in distress, like a woman in labor. At that time, they will see the Son of Man coming in a cloud with power and great glory. When these things begin to happen, they are to lift up their heads because their redemption is drawing near. Jesus tells them that when these things begin to happen, they are to lift up their heads because their redemption is drawing near. Jesus speaks of a time when Jerusalem will be surrounded by armies, and those within the city walls will be in distress, like a woman in labor. At that time, they will see the Son of Man coming in a cloud with power and great glory. When these things begin to happen, they are to lift up their heads because their redemption is drawing near. Jesus tells them that when these things begin to happen, they are to lift up their heads because their redemption is drawing near. Jesus speaks of a time when Jerusalem will be surrounded by armies, and those within the city walls will be in distress, like a woman in labor. At that time, they will see