Jesus came from Galilee to the River Jordan. And he wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to prevent him. And he said to Jesus, "I need to be baptized by you. Why do you come to me?"
Jesus answered, "Let it be so now. It is right for us to do this. It fulfills the plan of the Holy God." Then John agreed.
And immediately Jesus was baptized, he went up from the water. And at that moment the heavens were opened. And Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending upon him like a dove.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased."
Jesus is being tried.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness. And there the devil tempted him.
After forty days and forty nights of fasting, Jesus became hungry.
The tempter came to him and said, "If you are the Son of God, command these stones to become loaves of bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that comes from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city. He put him on the highest point of the Temple.
He said, "If you are the Son of God, throw yourself down. For it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you to guard you closely; they will carry you in their arms, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' " (Psalm 91:11,12)
Jesus answered, "It is written, 'Do not test the Lord your God.'" (Deuteronomy 6:16)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. He showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
He said: "If you prostrate yourselves to me and worship me, I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Go away, Satan! It is written, 'You shall worship the Lord your God, and Him only you shall serve.'" (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil left Jesus. And behold, angels came and ministered to him. Jesus begins to preach.
John was arrested and imprisoned. When Jesus heard this, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth. He went to live in the city of Capernaum. It was located beside the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
And thus what the prophet Isaiah said was fulfilled. He had said:
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee, where non-Jews dwell! Land by the Mediterranean Sea! Territory east of the Jordan River!
The people who dwell in deep darkness will see a great light. For they are dwelling now in a very dark land. But a light will shine on them." (Isaiah 9:1, 2)
From that time Jesus began to preach, saying, "Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand." Jesus' choice of the first disciples.
One day, Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
And immediately they left their nets and followed him.
And from there, he saw two other brothers. They were James and his son Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. And while they were mending their nets, Jesus called out to them.
Immediately they left the boat, their father, and followed Jesus. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus went throughout Galilee. There he taught in their synagogues. He preached the good news of God's kingdom. He healed every disease and illness among the people.
His fame spread throughout Syria. People brought him everyone who was sick with various diseases. Some were suffering from intense pain. Others were possessed by demons. Some were shaking violently. Others could not move at all. And Jesus healed them all.
A great multitude followed him. Some people came from Galilee, from the region of the Decapolis, from Jerusalem and Judea, and from beyond the Jordan.
Jesus gives blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds. He went up on the mountain and sat down. His disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. He said,
Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted.
Blessed are those who have renounced pride. They will be heirs to the earth.
Blessed are the hungry and thirsty for righteousness, for they will be filled.
Blessed are the merciful, for they will be shown mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called the children of God.
Blessed are those who are persecuted for the sake of righteousness, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are you when people hate you and when they exclude you and say all kinds of evil against you falsely for my sake.
Rejoice and be exceedingly glad. For your reward is great in heaven. Likewise, people rejected the prophets who lived long ago. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt loses its saltiness, how can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything, but is thrown out and trampled underfoot.
You are the light of the world. A city set on a hill cannot be hidden.
Neither do people light a lamp and put it under a bowl. Instead, they put it on a lampstand, and it gives light to everyone in the house.
Furthermore, let your light shine before others, so that they may see your good deeds and give glory to your Father in heaven. Jesus fulfills the law.
Do not suppose that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I have not come to abolish but to complete.
What I am determined to say is the truth. Heaven and earth will pass away before even one stroke of the Law is missing. Not even the smallest stroke of the Law will be missing until all is accomplished.
Do not neglect even the least of the commandments, nor teach others to neglect them. For if you do, you will be considered the least in the Kingdom of Heaven. But strive and teach these commandments, and you will be considered great in the Kingdom of Heaven.
Behold what I say to you. You must be more righteous than the scribes and Pharisees. If you are not, you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven. Murder.
You have heard what was said to those who lived in ancient times: 'You shall not murder. (Exodus 20:13) Whoever murders will be held accountable for that crime.'
But this is what I tell you. Do not be angry with your brother. If someone is angry with their brother, they are under judgment. And again, anyone who says to their brother, "Raca!" is to be brought before the council. But anyone who says, "You fool!" will be in danger of hellfire.
When you are presenting your offering at the altar and you remember that your brother has something against you.
Leave your offering before the altar. First, go and be reconciled to your brother. Then come and offer your gift.
Let's suppose that someone has filed a claim against you and is leading you to court. Settle the matter quickly. Do it while you are still with him on your way. If you don't, he may hand you over to the judge. And the judge may hand you over to the officer. And you may be thrown into prison.
What I intend to say is the truth. You are not leaving until you pay the last penny! Adultery.
You have heard that it was said, "You shall not commit adultery." (Exodus 20:14)
But that is what I am telling you. Do not look at a woman in a wrong way. Whoever does that has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. For it is better for you to lose one part of your body than for your whole body to be thrown into hell.
And if your right hand causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away from you. Because your hand is just one part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body go to hell. Divorce.
It was said, "When a man marries a woman and she displeases him and he gives her a certificate of divorce" (Deuteronomy 24:1).
But this is what I tell you. Whoever divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery. And whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. A man may not divorce his wife unless she has been unfaithful. Oaths.
Again, you have heard what was said to your people long ago. They were told, "Do not break the covenant you made with the Lord your God. Fulfill the promises you made to Him."
But that's what I'm telling you. Do not make any promises like that at all. Do not make them in the name of heaven. That is God's throne.
Do not call it by the name of the earth. That is the place of God's feet. Do not call it by the name of Jerusalem. That is the city of the great King.
Do not swear by your head. You cannot make even a single hair white or black.
Let your answer be "Yes" yes, and "No" no. And everything that exceeds that is from evil. Be kind to others.
You have heard that it was said, "Eye for eye, and tooth for tooth." (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21)
But that's what I'm telling you. Don't fight an evil person. "If someone strikes you on the right cheek, turn to him the other cheek also."
If someone sues you to take your shirt, let them have your coat as well.
If someone forces you to go one mile, go with them two miles.
Give to anyone who asks you for something. Do not turn away someone who wants to borrow something from you. Love your enemies.
You have heard that it was said, "Love your neighbor and hate your enemy." (Leviticus 19:18)
But I tell you, love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you.
So you will be called children of the Father in heaven. "He causes his sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the righteous and the unrighteous."
If you love those who love you, what reward will you get? Even tax collectors do that.
And if you only greet yourselves, what do you do more than others? Even those who do not believe do that.
Be perfect, therefore, as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Giving to those in need.
Beware of doing righteous deeds before people so that they may not be seen. Do not do that so that you may be praised by them. For your Father who is in the heavens does not reward you.
When you give to the needy, do not announce it with trumpets. Do not be like the hypocrites, who love to pray standing in the synagogues and in the streets, so that they may be honored by men. I tell you the truth, they have received their full reward.
When you give to charity, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
So your charity will be in secret. And your Father will reward you. For He sees what you do in secret. Prayer.
When you pray, do not be like the hypocrites, for they love to pray while standing in the synagogues and at the street corners, so that they may be seen by men. I tell you the truth, they have received their full reward.
When you pray, go into your room and close your door and pray to your Father who is in secret. And your Father will reward you. Your Father sees what you do in secret.
When you pray, do not babble on as the Gentiles do, for they think that they will be heard because of their many words.
Do not be like them. Your father knows what you need before you ask him.
This is how you should pray. "Our Father who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name.
Let your kingdom come. Let your will be done on earth as it is in heaven.
Give us this day our daily bread.
Forgive us our trespasses as we forgive those who trespass against us.
Deliver us from evil.
Forgive people when they wrong you. If you do, your Father in heaven will also forgive you.
But if you do not forgive people their sins, neither will your Father forgive your sins. Fast.
When you fast, do not look somber like the hypocrites do, who try to show their good deeds to other people. They disfigure their faces so that people may see that they are fasting. I tell you the truth: they have received their full reward.
But when you fast, anoint your head with olive oil, and wash your face.
So that people may not know that you are fasting, except your Father who is in heaven. And your Father who sees what is done in secret will reward you. Gather for yourselves treasures in heaven.
Do not store up for yourselves treasures on earth, where moth and rust destroy, and where thieves break in and steal.
Instead of that, save up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust consume, and where thieves do not break in nor steal.
Your heart will be where your treasures are.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. If your eyes are healthy, your whole body will be filled with light.
But if your eyes are bad, your whole body will be full of darkness. If the light within you is darkness, how great is that darkness!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either you will hate one and love the other, or you will be devoted to one and despise the other. You cannot serve both God and money. So don't be worried.
I tell you, do not worry. Do not worry about your life, what you will eat or drink, and do not worry about your body, what you will wear. Is life more than food? And is the body more than clothing?
Look at the birds of the air; for they neither sow nor reap, nor gather into barns. Yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not of more value than they?
Can you add one hour to your life by worrying?
And why do you care about clothing? Look how the wildflowers grow. They do not work, nor do they make clothing.
But that is what I am saying to you. Solomon, in all his glory, was not arrayed like one of those flowers.
If this is how God clothes the grass of the field, won't He clothe you even better? For the grass is here today and tomorrow is thrown into the fire. Your faith is too small!
Therefore, do not worry. Do not say, "What will we eat?" or "What will we drink?" or "What will we wear?"
Those who do not fear God run after all those things. Your Father who is in heaven knows that you need them.
But seek first the kingdom of God and his righteousness, and all these things will be added to you.
Therefore do not worry about tomorrow. Tomorrow will take care of itself. Each day has enough trouble of its own.
Be just when you judge others.
Do not judge others, for you will not be judged.
You will be judged by the same standard you judge others. You will be measured by the same measure you measure others.
You look at the publishing crumb in your friend's eye. But you don't pay attention to the wooden beam in your own eye.
How can you say to your friend, "Let me take the wooden splinter out of your eye"? How can you say this when there is a piece of wood in your own eye?
O demonstrator! First, take the wooden beam out of your own eye. Then you will be able to see clearly to take the dust mote out of your friend's eye.
Do not give the holy to Daevas. Nor cast pearls before swine. Lest they trample them under their feet, and turning, rend you. Ask, seek, knock.
Ask and it will be given to you. Seek and you will find. Knock and it will be opened to you.
Everyone who asks will receive. Whoever seeks will find. And the door will be opened for whoever knocks.
Let's assume that your son is asking for bread. Which of you will give him a stone?
Or let's suppose that he asked about a fish. Which of you would give him a serpent?
And if you are evil, you know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more will your Father in heaven give good gifts to those who ask him!
In everything, do to people what you would have them do to you. This is what was written in the Torah and the Prophets. The great and small gates.
Enter the kingdom of God through the narrow gate. Wide is the gate and broad is the road that leads to destruction. And many enter through it.
But the gate is narrow and the way is wide that leads to life. Few are those who find it. A tree and its fruit.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You can know them by their deeds. "Do people gather grapes from thorny bushes, or figs from thistles?"
In the same way, every good tree bears good fruit; but a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree does not bear fruit except that which is good, and a bad tree does not bear fruit except that which is bad.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
Every tree is known by its fruit.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only the one who does the will of my Father in heaven.
Many will say to me on that day: 'Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not cast out demons in your name? Did we not perform many miracles in your name?'
Then I will tell them clearly, 'I never knew you. Away from me, you evildoers!' The builders, the wise and the foolish.
So then, everyone who hears these words of mine and does them will be like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
The sky is raining. The water is rising. The wind is blowing and hitting that house. But it is not falling. It is built on the rock.
But everyone who hears my words and does not do them is like a foolish man who built his house on the sand.
The sky is raining. The water is rising. The wind is blowing and hitting that house. And it falls with a loud, booming sound."
Jesus finished saying all these things. The crowds were amazed at his teaching.
He taught like a sultan, not like their teachers.
Jesus heals a man with a skin disease.
Jesus went down from the mountain. And great multitudes followed him.
A man with a skin disease came and knelt before Jesus and said, "Lord, if you are willing to make me 'clean,' then you can do it."
Jesus stretched out his hand and touched the man. He said, "I want to heal him!" And he commanded, "Be clean!" And immediately the leprosy left him.
Jesus said to him, "Do not tell anyone, but go and show yourself to the priest and offer the sacrifice that Moses commanded. This will be a testimony to them." A Roman centurion believed.
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him. He asked Jesus for help.
He said, "Oh Lord, Your servant is lying down in the house and cannot move. He is suffering intense torment."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain answered, 'Lord, I am not worthy for you to come under my roof. But just say the word, and my servant will be healed.'
I myself am a man under authority. I have soldiers who obey my commands. I say to one, 'Go,' and he goes. And I say to another, 'Come,' and he comes. And I say to my servant, 'Do this,' and he does it."
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed. He said to those who were following him, "I tell you the truth: in all of Israel, I have not found such great faith."
I tell you that many will come from the east and the west. They will take their places at the feast in the kingdom of heaven. They will be seated with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.
But those who think they belong to the kingdom will be cast out into the darkness. There they will weep and grind their teeth."
Jesus said to the centurion, "Go! It will be done for you as you have believed." And the servant was healed at that moment. Jesus heals many.
When Jesus entered Peter's house, he saw his mother-in-law. She was lying down in bed, suffering from a fever.
Jesus touched her hand, and she was healed of her fever. She got up and began to serve him.
And when evening came, they brought to Him many who were possessed by demons; and He cast out the spirits by a word, and healed all who were sick.
He did this to fulfill what the prophet Isaiah had said: "He was wounded for our transgressions, He was bruised for our iniquities; The chastisement for our peace was upon Him; And by His stripes we are healed." (Isaiah 53:4) Following Christ has a price.
Jesus saw the crowd surrounding him. He ordered his disciples to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then the teacher of the law came to him and said to him, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered, "The foxes have holes, and the birds of the sky have nests, but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay his head."
And another disciple said to him, "Lord, let me first go and bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow me, and let the dead bury their own dead." Jesus rebuked the storm.
Jesus entered the boat. His disciples followed him.
Suddenly, a fierce storm swept over the lake. The waves beat against the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
The students went and woke him up. They said, "Lord, save us! We are drowning!"
He answered, "It is because of your little faith! Why are you so afraid?" And Jesus got up and rebuked the winds and the waves, and they became perfectly calm.
The students were amazed. They asked, "Who is this man? Even the winds and waves obey him!" Jesus heals two men possessed by demons.
Jesus arrived at the other side of the lake, to the region of the Gadarenes. He was met by two men who were controlled by demons. They came out of the tombs. The two men were so savage that no one could pass by that way.
«O son of God, what do you want from us?» they cried. «Have you come to torment us before our time comes?»
It was not far, a large herd of pigs was grazing.
The demons begged Jesus, saying, "If you cast us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" So the demons came out of the men and entered the pigs. The entire herd of pigs then rushed down the steep hillside and into the lake. They drowned in the water.
Those who were tending the pigs fled. They went to the city and told all this. They told the people what had happened to the men who were being controlled by demons.
Then all the people of the city went out to welcome Jesus. And when they saw him, they begged him to leave their district.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
Jesus got into the boat. Then he crossed to the other side of the lake and reached his hometown.
Some people brought a man to him who was unable to walk. He was thrown on a bed. Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man: "Do not lose hope, my son. Your sins are forgiven."
So some of the scribes said to themselves, "This man is casting reckless slander!"
Jesus knew what they were thinking. So he said, "Why do you have evil thoughts in your hearts?"
Is it easier to be told, 'Your sins have been forgiven'? Or to be told, 'Get up and walk'?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then He said to the man who was paralyzed, "Get up! Pick up your mat and go home."
The man got up and went home.
When the crowds saw this, they were filled with awe. They praised God for giving such authority to humans. Jesus chooses Matthew.
As Jesus was going away from there, he saw a man named Matthew, who was sitting in the tax office. And Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And so Matthew rose up and followed him.
Later Jesus was having dinner at Matthew's house. Many tax collectors and "sinners" came and ate with Jesus and his disciples.
When the Pharisees saw this, they asked his disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard this. And he said, "It is not those who are well who need a doctor, but those who are sick."
Go and learn what this means: 'I desire mercy and not sacrifice.' (Hosea 6:6) 'I have not come to call the righteous but sinners.' They ask him about fasting.
One day, the disciples of John came. They said to Jesus, "We and the Pharisees fast, but your disciples do not fast."
Jesus answered, "How can the groomsmen mourn as long as the bridegroom is with them? The time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; then they will fast."
People don't patch old clothes with a piece of new cloth. The new piece will pull the old one apart. That will make the tear worse.
People don't pour new wine into old wine skins. If they do, the skins break. The wine spills out, and the skins are ruined. Instead, everyone pours new wine into new wine skins. This preserves both. A dead girl and a suffering woman.
While Jesus was saying this, a ruler came and knelt before him. "My daughter has just died," he said. "But come and put your hand on her, and she will live."
Jesus rose up and went away with him. And so did his disciples.
And at that moment, a woman approached Jesus from behind. She had been suffering from bleeding for 12 years. She touched the edge of his robe.
"I thought, 'All I need to do is touch his clothes. Then I will be healed.'"
Jesus turned and saw her. He said to her, "Woman, do not lose hope." "Your faith has healed you." The woman was healed at that moment.
When Jesus entered the governor's house, he saw the flute players there. And he saw the noisy crowd.
He said, "Go away. The girl is not dead. She is sleeping." But they laughed at him.
After the crowds were sent out, Jesus went in. He took the little girl by the hand, and she got up.
News spread about all that Jesus had done in that region. Jesus heals two blind men.
And as Jesus continued on his way from there, two blind men followed him. They cried out, saying, "Have mercy on us, O Son of David!"
When Jesus went inside, the blind came to him. He asked them, "Do you believe that I can do this?" They answered, "Yes, Lord."
Then he touched their eyes. And he said, "Be it to you as you have believed."
Now they could see again. And Jesus sternly warned them, "Be careful that no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news. They talked about it everywhere in that area.
And as they were coming out, another man was brought to Jesus. An evil spirit was in him, and he could not speak.
When the devil was cast out, the man spoke. The multitude marveled, saying, "Such a thing was never seen in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, 'It is by the prince of demons that he drives out demons.' There are few hired hands.
Jesus was hungry in all the cities and villages. He was teaching in their synagogues, preaching the gospel of the kingdom of God, and healing every disease and sickness.
When he saw the crowds, he felt a deep concern for them. They were defeated and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is great, but the laborers are few."
Ask the Lord of the harvest to send out workers into his harvest.
Jesus sends the twelve disciples.
Jesus called his twelve disciples to come to him. And he gave them authority to cast out demons, and to heal every disease and every illness.
Here are the names of the twelve apostles. First is Simon Peter, and his brother Andrew. Then is James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John.
Then come Philip and Bartholomew, and also Thomas and Matthew the tax collector. And there are also James the son of Alphaeus and Thaddaeus.
The last two are Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas is the one who would later betray Jesus to his enemies.
Jesus sent these twelve out with the following instructions: "Do not go to the Gentiles," he said. "Nor enter any Samaritan town."
Instead, go to the Children of Israel. They are like stray sheep.
And as you go, preach this message: 'The Kingdom of Heaven is near.'
Heal the sick. Raise the dead. Cleanse those with leprosy. Drive out demons. It has been freely given to you, so freely give.
You shall not carry gold, nor silver, nor copper in your wallets.
Do not take a bag on the trip. Do not take extra clothes, sandals, or a walking stick. The operator must be given what is needed.
When you enter a town or village, look for someone willing to receive you. Stay with this person until you leave.
Upon entering the house, greet those who are in it.
If that house welcomes you, give it your peace. And if it does not, do not bless it.
Some people may not welcome you or listen to what you have to say. If they do not, shake the dust from your feet as you leave that house or town.
What I am about to say is the truth. On the day of judgment, it will be easier for Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city.
I send you out as sheep among wolves. Therefore be wise as serpents and harmless as doves.
Beware! People will hand you over to local courts. They will scourge you in their synagogues.
You will be brought before rulers and kings because of me. You will be witnesses for them and for those who are not Jews.
But when you are arrested, do not worry about what you will say or how you will say it. At that time, the right words to say will be given to you.
It will not be you who speaks, but the spirit of your father who will speak through you.
Brothers will deliver each other to be killed. Fathers will deliver their sons. Sons will rise up against their fathers and kill them.
Everyone will hate you because of me. But whoever endures to the end will be saved.
When people attack you in one place, flee to another. What I am about to tell you is true. You will not finish going through the towns of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
The student is not better than his teacher. And the servant is not better than his master.
It is enough for the student to be like his teacher, and it is enough for the servant to be like his master. If the master of the house has been called Beelzebul, what will the rest who dwell in it expect?
Therefore, fear not your enemies. For everything that is hidden will be revealed, and everything that is concealed will be brought to light.
What I tell you in secret, speak in public. What is whispered in your ear, proclaim from the rooftops.
Fear not those who kill the body and cannot kill the soul; rather, fear the One who is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.
Are not two sparrows sold for a farthing? Yet not one of them falls to the ground apart from your Father's knowledge.
He even counts every single hair on your head!
Do not fear. You are better than many sparrows.
What about someone who says in front of others that they know me? I will also say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I know him.
But what about someone who says in front of others that they don't know me? I will say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I don't know him.
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to set son against his father, daughter against her mother, and daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law.
The enemies of a man are those of his own household.' (Micah 7:6)
He who loves his father or mother more than me is not worthy of me. And he who loves his son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me.
He who does not bear his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
Whoever finds his life will lose it, and whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.
He who welcomes you welcomes me, and he who welcomes me welcomes the one who sent me.
If someone receives a prophet with a prophecy, he will receive the reward of a prophet. If someone receives a righteous man with righteousness, he will receive the reward of a righteous man.
If someone gives any of these little ones just a cup of cold water, truly I tell you, he will certainly be rewarded."
Jesus and John the Baptist.
Jesus finished his teaching to his twelve disciples. Then he went out teaching and preaching in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard what Christ was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who is to come, or shall we look for someone else?"
Jesus answered, "Go and tell John what you hear and what you see."
The blind receive their sight. The lame walk. Those with skin diseases are healed. The deaf hear. The dead are raised to life. And the gospel is preached to the poor.
Blessed are those who have not abandoned their faith because of me."
While John's disciples were leaving, Jesus began to speak to the crowds about John. He said, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?"
If it is not so, what did you go out to see? A man in fine clothing? No. Those who wear fine clothing are in kings' palaces.
So what did you go out into the desert to see? A prophet? I tell you, yes, more than a prophet.
He is the one about whom it was written in the book. It says: "'Behold, I send My messenger to prepare the way before Me.'" (Malachi 3:1)
What I am about to tell you is the truth. No one has ever been born who is greater than John the Baptist. But the least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he is.
From the days of John the Baptist, the kingdom of heaven has been forcefully advancing. And violent people take it by force.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John came.
If you are willing to accept that, then John is Elijah who was to come.
Let him who has an ear listen.
To what can I compare the people of today? They are like children, sitting in the marketplace, calling out. They say:
We played for you on the flute, but you did not dance. We sang a funeral song, but you were not sad.
When John came, he did not eat or drink, as you eat and drink. And people would say, "He has a demon!"
But when the Son of Man came, he ate and drank as you do. And people said, 'This glutton and drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners!' Yet wisdom is vindicated by her deeds. Cities that did not repent of their sins.
Jesus began to speak out against the cities where He had performed most of His miracles. The people of those cities had not repented of their sins. And He said:
Oh, what misery awaits you, Chorazin! Oh, what misery awaits you, Bethsaida! If the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long ago. They would have worn sackcloth, and sat in ashes.
But I tell you this. On the day of judgment, Tyre and Sidon will be more tolerable to you than this place.
And you, Capernaum, will you be exalted to heaven? No! But you will be brought down to Hades. If the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Sodom, it would have remained to this day.
But I tell you this. The day of judgment will be more tolerable for Sodom than for you. Take comfort, all you who are weary.
At that time Jesus said, "I praise you, Father. You are the Lord of heaven and earth. You have hidden these things from the wise and learned. But you have shown them to those who are little."
Yes, Dad. This is what you wanted.
My father gave me everything. The Father is the only one who knows the Son. And the only ones who know the Father are the Son and those whom the Son chooses to reveal Him.
Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
Be my slaves and learn from me. I am gentle and empty of arrogance. You will find rest for your souls.
My service is easy, and my burden is light.
Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
One Sabbath day, Jesus was going through the fields. And his disciples were hungry. So they began to pluck ears of wheat and eat them.
When the Pharisees saw this, they said to Jesus, "Look, it is against the law to do these things on the Sabbath. Yet your disciples are doing it!"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read what David did when he and his companions were hungry"?
So then he entered the house of God. And he ate, he and his men, the consecrated bread. Only the priests were permitted to eat it.
Have you not read the law? It is written how priests in the temple should do their work every Sabbath day, but they are not considered guilty.
I tell you that which is greater than the temple is here.
The sacred writings say: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice." (Hosea 6:6) You do not know the meaning of these words. If you knew them, you would not file lawsuits against innocent people.
The son of man is Lord of the Sabbath.
Then Jesus went from there and entered their synagogue.
There was a man who had a withered and deformed hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They asked him, saying, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?"
He said to them, "What if one of your sheep fell into a pit on the Sabbath? Would you not reach in and lift it out?"
Man is of more value than sheep! Therefore, the law is allowing us to do good on the Sabbath day."
Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and it was restored, just as sound as the other.
But the Pharisees went out and conspired to kill Jesus, the chosen servant of God.
Jesus was aware of all the schemes of the Pharisees. Therefore, He left that place. Many followed Him, and He healed all their sick.
But he warned them not to tell anyone about his identity.
That was so that what the prophet Isaiah said would come true: he says,
Here is my servant, whom I choose, him who I love. I am very well pleased with him. I will put my Spirit upon him, and he will call to the nations that everything will be repaired.
He will not argue or shout. No one will hear his voice in the streets.
He will not break a twig that is already bent. He will not extinguish a flame that is already dim. He will fix everything.
You will place your hope in him." (Isaiah 42:1-4) Jesus and Beelzebul
A man who was possessed by demons, and who was blind and unable to speak, was brought to Jesus. Jesus healed the man, and then he was able to speak and see.
All the people were amazed and said, "Can this be the Son of David?"
When the Pharisees heard this, they said, "This fellow drives out demons by the prince of demons."
Jesus knew their thoughts. He said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself is laid waste; and every city or house divided against itself will not stand."
Satan only drives out Satan, so he fights against himself. How then can his kingdom stand?
Are you saying that I cast out demons by the power of Beelzebul? By whose power, then, do your people cast out demons? Then they would be the rulers over you.
But if I cast out demons by the finger of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or consider this. How can you break into a powerful man's house and simply take what he owns? You must first tie him up. Then you can rob his house.
He who is not with me is against me, and he who does not gather with me scatters.
Here is what I say to you. Every sin and every evil word spoken against God will be forgiven. But speaking evil things against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
Whoever speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven. But whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven, either in this age or in the age to come.
If you improve a tree, its fruit will be good. And if you corrupt a tree, its fruit will be bad. You can know the tree by its fruit.
O nest of poisonous vipers! How can those who are evil say anything good? For out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaks.
The good man says good things. These stem from the goodness hidden within him. And the evil man says evil things. These stem from the evil hidden within him.
But this is what I am telling you. On the Day of Judgment, people will have to answer for every thoughtless word they spoke.
In your own words you will be condemned or acquitted." The verse of the miraculous Jonah.
Some Pharisees and scribes came to Jesus. They said, "Teacher, we want to see a sign from you."
He answered, "The evil and unfaithful generation seeks a sign. But no sign will be given except the sign of Jonah the prophet."
Jonah was in the belly of a great fish three days and three nights. Something like this will happen to the Son of Man. He will be in the tomb three days and three nights.
The men of Nineveh will stand up on the day of judgment with this generation and will condemn it; for if the men of Nineveh had repented at the preaching of Jonah, how much more will the people of Nineveh rise up at the judgment with you, who have not repented, even though you have seen it all.
The Queen of the South will rise up on the day of judgment with the people who are now living. And she will condemn them. She came from a very great distance to hear the wisdom of Solomon. And now, here is someone greater than Solomon.
What happens when an unclean spirit goes out of a man? It passes through dry places, seeking rest, but finding none.
Then he says, "I will return to my house that I left." When he arrived there, he found the house empty. The house had been swept and tidied.
"Then the unclean spirit goes out and takes with it seven other spirits more wicked than itself. And they enter and dwell there. And the condition of that man becomes worse than the first. So will it be for this evil generation." Jesus' mother and brothers.
While Jesus was still speaking to the crowds, his mother and brothers stood outside. They wanted to speak to him.
Someone told him, "Your mother and brothers are standing outside. They want to speak to you."
Jesus answered and said to him, "Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples. He said, "Behold, my mother! These are my brothers!"
"Whoever does the will of my Father in heaven is my brother and sister and mother."
The Farmer's Story
And on that same day Jesus went out of the house and sat by the sea of Galilee.
Large crowds gathered around him. So he climbed into a boat. And he sat in it. And all the people stood on the shore.
Then he told them many stories. And he said, "A farmer went out to sow his seed.
Scattering the seeds on the ground. Some fell on a path. The birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell on rocky places, where there was not much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil was not deep.
When the sun rose, it burned the plants. They dried up because they had no roots.
Other seeds fell among the thorns; and the thorns grew up and choked the plants.
And some seed fell on good soil, and produced a crop thirty, sixty, or a hundredfold of what was planted.
Let him who has an ear listen.
The disciples came to him. They asked, "Why do you speak to the people in parables?"
He answered, "To you it has been given to know the secrets of the kingdom of the heavens; but to those outside, everything comes in parables.
Everyone who possesses this kind of knowledge will increase even more. Indeed, they will have a great deal. As for those who do not possess this kind of knowledge, even the little they have will be taken away from them.
Here's why I resort to stories when I talk to people. I say, "They are looking, but they don't really see. They are listening, but they don't really hear or understand."
In them are fulfilled the words of the prophet Isaiah. He said, "You will indeed hear, but you will not understand; and you will indeed see, but you will not perceive. For this people's heart has become callous; they have been hardened to the touch of God. And they, the prophets, keep saying to them, 'To hear, you will hear, and you will not understand; and to see, you will see, and you will not perceive. For the heart of this people has become dulled; they have shut their eyes, lest they see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their hearts, and turn and I would heal them.' But they said, 'Nothing will do for us unless perhaps he will stretch out his hand and strike us down, as one of the prophets used to do.' And as for me, ' said Isaiah, 'I have been filled with God's wrath; I am weary of holding it in. I will pour it out on the children before their very eyes; I will vent my anger on the people.' Isaiah also said, 'Let not the wise man glory in his wisdom; let not the mighty man glory in his might; let not the rich man glory in his riches; but let him glory in this, that he understands and knows Me, that I am the Lord, who exercises kindness, justice, and righteousness in the earth; for in these things I delight, declares the Lord. "
Their hearts have become callous. It is difficult for them to hear with their ears, and they have closed their eyes. Otherwise, they would see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their hearts, and return to the Lord, who would heal them.' (Isaiah 6:9,10)
But woe to your eyes because they see, and woe to your ears because they hear.
What I am about to tell you is true. Many prophets and righteous people wanted to see what you see. But they did not see it. They wanted to hear what you hear. But they did not hear it.
Listen! Here is the indication of the story of the peasant.
People hear the message of the kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the evil one comes. He steals what has been planted in their hearts. Those people are like seed sown along the path.
And others received the seed that fell on rocky places. These are the ones who hear the word and immediately receive it with joy.
But they have no root. Therefore they last only for a short time. They quickly abandon the faith when tribulation or suffering comes because of the word.
And others, the seeds fell among the thorns; and these are the ones who hear the word. But the cares of this world and the deceitfulness of riches choke it, and it bears no fruit.
But others received the seed that fell on good soil. These are the ones who hear the word and understand it. They produce a harvest, one hundred, sixty, or thirty times what the farmer planted." The Parable of the Sower and the Thorns.
Jesus told the crowds another parable. "Thus is the Kingdom of Heaven," He said. "A man sowed good seed in his field."
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat started to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
Servants of the Lord came to him and said, "Lord, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where then did this harmful seed come from?"
He said, "An enemy did this." The servants asked him, "Do you want us to go and pull out the weeds?"
"No," the owner replied. "While you are weeding, you might pull up the wheat along with it."
Let both of them grow together until the harvest. Then I will say to the workers, 'First, gather the weeds. Tie them into bundles to be burned. Then gather the wheat. Bring it into my storage.'" Stories about the mustard seed and the yeast.
Jesus told the multitudes another parable. He said, "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed. A man took the seed and planted it in a field."
It is the smallest of all your seeds. But when it grows, it is the largest of all the garden plants. It becomes a tree. And the birds come and make their nests in its branches."
And Jesus spoke to them another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed, which a man took and planted in his field. Though it is the smallest of all seeds, yet when it is grown, it is the greatest of all plants, and becomes a large tree, so that the birds of the air come and nest in its branches."
Jesus spoke all these things to the crowds in parables. He did not speak to them without a parable.
And the words which the prophet spoke were confirmed. For he had said, "I will open my mouth in parables. I will utter dark sayings, things hidden from the foundation of the world." (Psalm 78:2) Jesus explains the parable of the weeds.
Then Jesus went away from the crowds and went into the house. And his disciples came to him. And they said, "Explain to us the parable of the sower and the weeds."
He answered and said, 'The one who soweth the good seed is the son of man.'
The field is the world. And the good seed are those who belong to the kingdom of God. And the tares are those who belong to the evil one.
The enemy who sows them is the devil. And the harvest is the Day of Judgment. And the laborers are angels.
The weeds are pulled up and burned in the fire. This is how the Day of Judgment will be.
The Son of Man will send his angels. And they will remove from his kingdom all that causes sin. And they will also remove all who do evil.
They will throw them into the furnace of fire. There they will weep and grind their teeth.
Then the people of God will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Whoever has ears to hear, let him hear. The stories of the hidden treasure and the pearl.
The kingdom of heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. When found, it is hidden again, and the person rejoices over it. Then they go and sell all that they have and buy that field.
And again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant seeking fine pearls.
He found one that was very precious. So he went and sold everything he owned. And he bought that pearl. The Parable of the Net.
And again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net that was thrown into the sea and gathered fish of every kind.
When it was full, the fishermen pulled it ashore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. But they threw the bad fish away.
This will be the case on the Day of Resurrection. The angels will come. They will separate the people who did evil from those who did good.
The wicked will he fling into the burning fiery furnace. There shall the wicked wail and grind their teeth.
"Do you understand all these things?" Jesus asked. "Yes," they answered.
He said to them: "Every teacher of the Law who has been trained in the Kingdom of the Heavens is like a house owner who brings out of his treasure new and old things." A prophet without honor.
Jesus finished telling these stories. Then he left there.
He came to his hometown Nazareth. There he began to teach people in their synagogue. And they were astonished. And they said, "Where did this man get this wisdom? And where did he get this power to perform miracles?"
Isn't this the son of the carpenter? Isn't his mother called Mary? Aren't his brothers called James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all these things from?"
They never liked it. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own country, and not in his own house."
Few miracles were made there because they did not have faith.
John the Baptist's head is cut off.
In those days, Herod the tetrarch, being in Galilee, heard reports about Jesus.
He told his servants, "This is John the Baptist. He has been raised from the dead! This is why he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod arrested John. He bound him and imprisoned him in prison because of Herodias. She was the wife of Herod's brother, Philip.
John was saying to Herod, "It is against the law for you to have her."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they believed that John was a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias' daughter danced for him and his guests. She pleased Herod very much.
He promised her solemnly that he would give her what she asked for.
Her mother said to her, "What are you saying?" So the girl said to Herod, "I want the head of John the Baptist on a large plate."
The king was very upset. But he remembered his promise and his guests for dinner. So, he ordered one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod ordered John to be beheaded in prison.
His head was brought on a large platter and given to the maid. Then she carried it to her mother.
John's disciples came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds five thousand people.
Jesus heard what had happened to John. And he desired to be alone. So he went in a boat to a lonely place. The multitudes knew this, and they followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus came to the shore, he saw a great multitude. He was deeply moved with compassion for them. He healed their sick.
When evening had come, the disciples came to him, saying, "This is a desolate place, and it is now getting late. Send the crowds away to go into the surrounding countryside and villages, to buy for themselves food."
Jesus answered, "There is no need for them to go. You give them something to eat."
"We have nothing but five loaves of bread and two fish," they answered.
He said, "Bring them to me."
Then Jesus instructed the people to sit on the grass. He took five loaves of bread and two fish. He looked up to heaven and gave thanks. Then he broke the loaves and gave them to the disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. And the disciples picked up twelve baskets of leftovers.
About 5000 men ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on the water.
So Jesus told the disciples to immediately get into the boat and go on ahead of him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee. Then he sent the crowds away.
After dismissing them, he went up to the mountain alone to pray. And when evening came, he was there alone.
The boat was already far from land. The waves were hitting it hard because the wind was blowing against it.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to the disciples. And He walked on the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake and were terrified. "It's a ghost!" they cried out in fear.
Their Jesus immediately called out to them, "Be courageous! I am he. Do not be afraid."
"Lord, if it is you," Peter said. "Tell me to come to you on the water."
Jesus said, "Come." So Peter got out of the boat and walked on the water toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. He began to sink. So he cried out, "Lord, save me!"
Jesus stretched out his hand and grasped him, saying, "Your faith is too little! Why have you doubted me?"
When they climbed into the boat, the wind died down.
So those who had been in the boat worshiped Jesus and said, "Truly you are the Son of God!"
And they crossed over to the lake, and they anchored at Gennesaret.
The men who lived there recognized Jesus. So they sent word into all the surrounding countryside. People brought all their sick to Jesus.
They begged him to let the sick just touch the edge of his robe. And everyone who touched it was healed.
What makes people "impure"?
Some Pharisees and teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. They asked him:
Why do your disciples not live according to the traditions of the elders? For your disciples do not wash their hands before they eat!
Jesus answered: "And why do you not obey the command of God? But you prefer to follow your own teachings!"
The Lord said, "Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16) And He also said, "Whoever curses his father or his mother, he will surely be put to death." (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
But you let people tell their parents, "Whatever help anyone of us has given to you is a gift designated for God."
Therefore, they do not need to honor their parents with their gift. You nullify the word of God in order to follow your own teachings.
Protesters! Isaiah was right in prophesying about you. He said:
These people honor me with their words, but their hearts are far from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. They only know one thing: human laws." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowds to Him. And He said, "Listen and understand."
What goes into your mouth does not defile you, but what comes out of your mouth, that is what defiles you."
Then the disciples came to him. They asked him, "Do you know that the Pharisees were angry when they heard this?"
Jesus answered, "There are plants that my heavenly Father has not planted. They will be pulled up by the roots."
Leave them alone. For the Pharisees are blind guides. And if the blind leads the blind, both shall fall into the ditch."
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
"Do you still not understand?" Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Whatever goes into the mouth, goes into the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth are from the heart. Those are the things that make you "impure."
Evil thoughts proceed from the heart. So do murder, adultery, and sexual immorality. So do theft, false testimony, and lying about others.
Those are the things that make you "unclean." But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." "The Faith of a Canaanite Woman"
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
There was a woman from Canaan who lived near Tyre and Sidon. She came to him and cried out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on me! My daughter is tormented by a demon. She is suffering greatly."
Jesus did not say a word. His disciples came to Him. They begged Him saying, "Cast it out. It is screaming constantly behind us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman knelt before him. "Oh Lord! Save me!" she said.
He answered and said, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
Yes, Lord, she said. "But even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' table."
Jesus answered and said, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be to you as you desire." And her daughter was healed at that hour. Jesus feeds four thousand people.
Jesus departed from there. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up to the mountain and sat down.
Large crowds came to him. They brought the blind and the lame. And they also brought those who were paralyzed, and the mute, and many others. They laid them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people marveled. Those who could not speak were speaking. And the weak were healed. And the lame were walking. And the blind were seeing. So the people glorified the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to come to him. And he said, "I am deeply concerned about these people. They have already been with me three days, and they have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry. If I do, they will collapse on the way home."
His disciples answered him, saying, "There is nothing here but five loaves and two fish. Where would we get enough bread to satisfy such a large crowd?"
"How many loaves have you?" Jesus asked. "Seven," they answered, "and a few small fish."
Jesus said to the multitudes to sit down on the ground.
He took the seven loaves of bread and the fish, and He gave thanks. Then He broke them and gave them to the disciples, and the disciples distributed them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. And afterward, the disciples took up seven baskets of leftover fragments.
The number of men who ate was 40,000. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus had sent the crowds away, he got into the boat. And he went to the region of Magadan.
Jesus is asked for a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. And they asked him to show them a sign from heaven.
He replied: "In the evening you look at the sky. You say: 'The weather will be good. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning you say, "Today will be stormy. The sky is red and cloudy." You know what you see in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
An evil and untrustworthy generation seeks a sign, but no sign will be given to it except the sign of Jonah. And Jesus left them and went away. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The disciples crossed over to the other side of the lake. They had forgotten to bring bread.
"Beware," Jesus said to them. "Guard yourselves from the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The students discussed this among themselves. And they said: "He is saying this because we did not bring bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. He said, "O little faith! Why do you reason among yourselves, saying, 'We have no bread'?"
Don't you understand yet? Don't you remember the five loaves for five thousand? Don't you remember how many baskets of fragments you collected?
Don't you remember the seven loaves of bread for a thousand and a quarter? Don't you remember how many baskets were filled with pieces that you collected?
How can you not understand? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
At that point, the disciples understood that Jesus was not warning them about bread yeast. Rather, He was warning them about the teachings of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus went into the region of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "Who do people say the Son of Man is?"
They answered, "Some say John the Baptist; others, Elijah; still others, Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"But what about you?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Peter answered and said, "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven. And I tell you, you are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church, and all the powers of the underworld will not conquer it."
Here is what I say to you. You are Peter. And on this rock I will build my church. And the gates of Hades will not overcome it.
I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. Whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. And whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Christ. Jesus speaks of his coming death.
From that time on, Jesus began to explain to his disciples what would happen to him. He told them, "I must go to Jerusalem. There I will be arrested, tried, and sentenced to death by crucifixion. After three days, I will rise from the dead."
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "God forbid, Lord!" he said. "This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to me. You do not have in mind the concerns of God, but merely human concerns."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples. He said, "If anyone wants to follow me, they must deny themselves, take up their cross, and follow me."
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What good would it do a man to gain the whole world and lose his own soul? Or what could a man give in exchange for his soul?
Truly, the Son of Man is about to come in the glory of His Father with His angels, and He will repay each person according to his deeds.
What I am about to say is true. Some who are standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.
Jesus' appearance changed.
And after six days, Jesus took Peter and John, James's brother, up on a high mountain. And they were all alone.
And there before him his form was altered. And his face shone like the sun. And his clothes became white as light.
And in that moment, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. And Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will make here for you three dwellings. One for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
While Peter was still speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them, and a voice from the cloud said, "This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased. Listen to him!"
When the disciples heard this, they were exceedingly afraid. They fell on their faces and prostrated themselves.
But Jesus came and touched them. He said, "Get up. Do not be afraid."
When they raised their eyes, they saw no one except Jesus.
They went down the mountain. And on their way down, Jesus told them what to do. "Do not tell anyone," he said, "what you have seen. Wait until the Son of Man has been raised from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why then do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "This is correct. Elijah is the one who is about to come and renew all things."
But I tell you, Elijah has already come, and people did not recognize him. And they did to him everything they wanted to do. So also the Son of Man will suffer at their hands."
At this the disciples understood that Jesus was speaking about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy possessed by a demon.
As they came near the crowd, a man came to Jesus. He knelt down before him.
He said, "O Lord, have mercy on my son. He shivers violently and suffers greatly. He often falls into the fire or into the water."
I brought him to your disciples, but they could not heal him.
«O ye of little faith and evil!» Jesus answered. «How long shall I bear with you? How long shall I suffer you? Bring me the child.»
Jesus commanded the demon to come out of the boy, and it came out of him. He was healed at that moment.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately, and asked, "Why could we not cast him out?"
He answered, "Because your faith is exceedingly small. What I am about to tell you is the truth. If you had faith as small as a mustard seed, it would be enough. You could say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it would move. Nothing would be impossible for you."
.<.>
They met in Galilee. Then Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man is to be delivered into the hands of men."
They will kill him. And on the third day, he will rise from the dead." The disciples were filled with grief. Jesus pays the temple tax.
Jesus and his disciples reached Capernaum. There, the tax collectors came to Peter. They asked him, "Doesn't your teacher pay the temple tax?"
"Yes, he does that," he answered. And when Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "From whom do the kings of the earth take toll and tax? From their own sons or from others?"
"From others," Peter answered. "Then the children ought not to pay," Jesus said to him.
But we don't want to anger them. So go to the lake and cast your fishing line. Take the first fish you catch. Open its mouth. You will find there exactly the coin you need. Take it and give it to them as a tax for you and me.
